Chapter 4: Design and Implementation

4.1 Introduction

This chapter describes the design and implementation of the phishing detection system developed for this dissertation. The system is implemented as an experimental pipeline rather than a production deployment tool, because the aim of the study is to compare the effect of class imbalance treatment techniques on machine learning-based phishing website detection. The design therefore focuses on reproducibility, fair comparison and controlled evaluation across multiple datasets and classifiers.Draft-Final-Version.docx

The implementation follows the methodology established in the previous chapter. In particular, the system is structured so that imbalance treatment methods are applied only to the training data, while the test data remain untouched until final evaluation. This approach ensures that the results reflect genuine model performance rather than artefacts introduced by data leakage or inconsistent preprocessing.machinelearningmasteryDraft-Final-Version.docx

4.2 System Architecture

The system is organised as a modular pipeline with five main stages: data loading, preprocessing, imbalance treatment, model training and performance evaluation. This structure supports consistent evaluation because each imbalance treatment technique is assessed under the same overall experimental conditions. It also makes the workflow easier to reproduce and extend, since each stage can be adjusted independently without altering the rest of the pipeline.Draft-Final-Version.docx

The design was intentionally kept simple and modular because the dissertation is focused on controlled experimentation rather than on building a production-grade phishing detection tool. A modular pipeline is suitable for this purpose because it allows the same logic to be reused across all experiment combinations. It also makes it straightforward to add future techniques or classifiers if the project is extended later.Draft-Final-Version.docx

Figure 4.1 System architecture of the phishing detection pipeline.
Source: Author's own work.

4.3 Dataset Design

Dataset selection was governed by a requirement that follows directly from the research question. Because this study evaluates techniques for treating class imbalance, the datasets themselves must exhibit class imbalance. Several widely cited phishing benchmarks do not: the UCI Phishing Websites dataset contains 44.31% phishing instances (an imbalance ratio of approximately 1:1.26), and the benchmark of Hannousse and Yahiouche is balanced by design at exactly 50% (1:1.00). Applied to such data, oversampling methods have almost nothing to correct, and cost-sensitive weighting reduces to no treatment at all because the computed class weights approach unity. Both datasets were therefore examined and rejected as unsuitable for this particular study, although they remain valuable benchmarks for classification accuracy more generally.

The first dataset used is that of Vrbančič et al. (2020), which contains 88,647 instances described by 111 numeric features extracted from the URL, the domain and the hosting infrastructure. Of these instances, 30,647 are phishing and 58,000 are legitimate, giving 34.57% phishing and an imbalance ratio of approximately 1:1.89. The dataset is complete and contains no missing values.

The second dataset is URL-Phish (2025), which contains 116,600 URLs described by 22 numeric lexical and structural features such as URL length, domain length, digit ratio, entropy and HTTPS usage. It comprises 16,600 phishing and 100,000 legitimate instances, giving 14.24% phishing and an imbalance ratio of approximately 1:6.02. The phishing samples were drawn from the PhishTank repository between November 2024 and September 2025, making this a substantially more recent benchmark than most phishing datasets in circulation.

It should be noted that the publication accompanying URL-Phish describes 111,660 URLs of which 11,660 are phishing, whereas the published data file contains 116,600 rows of which 16,600 are phishing. The counts reported here were measured directly from the distributed file and are the figures used throughout this study.

Using these two datasets strengthens the design in three respects. First, both are imbalanced as published, so the imbalance treatment techniques operate on genuine skew rather than on an artificially constructed distribution. Second, the two datasets differ substantially in the severity of that skew, at approximately 1:1.89 and 1:6.02, which permits analysis of whether the effectiveness of a technique depends on how severe the imbalance is. Third, they differ considerably in feature richness, at 111 and 22 features respectively, which supports cross-dataset interpretation and reduces the likelihood that conclusions are specific to a single feature representation.

Both datasets are large relative to the computational cost of the experimental design. Because the Support Vector Machine has approximately quadratic complexity in the number of training instances, and because the full comparison matrix is repeated across three random seeds, each dataset is reduced to 20,000 instances by stratified sampling before the experiments are run. Sampling is proportional within each class, so the natural imbalance ratio is preserved exactly: the reduced Vrbančič data retains 34.57% phishing instances and the reduced URL-Phish data retains 14.23%. Only the volume of data is reduced; the class distribution under study is unaffected.

